  "balancing-studies-real-projects": {
    id: "6",
    title: "Balancing Studies and Real Projects",
    slug: "balancing-studies-real-projects",
    excerpt:
      "As a Computer Science student, managing academic load and real-world projects is tricky. Here's how I balance both — planning, learning from mentors, and turning coursework into portfolio-ready work.",
    content: `# Balancing Studies and Real Projects

Being a Computer Science student while building real projects is hard. There's coursework, exams, assignments — and then the projects you actually want to build. Here's how I manage both without burning out.

## The Challenge

### Academic Workload
- Lectures (20 hrs/week)
- Assignments (10 hrs/week)
- Exam prep (varies)
- Group projects (unpredictable)

### Personal Projects
- TicketPilot (AI support assistant)
- CineReads (movie recommendation app)
- ScrubPy (data cleaning library)
- This portfolio site

**Total:** 50–60 hrs/week if I'm not careful.

## My System

### 1. **Time Blocking**

I use Google Calendar religiously:

**Weekdays:**
- 9 AM – 1 PM: Classes
- 2 PM – 4 PM: Assignments/coursework
- 7 PM – 10 PM: Personal projects

**Weekends:**
- Saturday: Deep work on one big project
- Sunday: Buffer for catch-up + rest

**Rules:**
- No project work during exam weeks
- One "project-free" day per week (usually Sunday afternoon)

### 2. **Align Projects with Coursework**

This is the secret. When possible, I turn assignments into portfolio pieces.

**Examples:**
- **Database Systems course** → Built the backend for CineReads (PostgreSQL, indexing, query optimization)
- **Web Development course** → Submitted this portfolio as my final project
- **Machine Learning course** → Used the concepts to build TicketPilot's AI features

**Benefits:**
- Double ROI on your time
- Better grades (you're motivated to do good work)
- Professors love real-world applications

**How to pitch it:**
> "For my final project, I'd like to build a full-stack app that demonstrates the concepts we've learned. Can I document the process as my submission?"

Most professors say yes.

### 3. **Prioritize Ruthlessly**

Not every idea needs to be built. I use this framework:

| Priority | Criteria | Examples |
|----------|----------|----------|
| **High** | Aligns with career goals | FastAPI apps, AI projects |
| **Medium** | Interesting but not urgent | Experimental tech (Rust, Go) |
| **Low** | "Cool idea" but no clear benefit | Yet another todo app |

I only work on High priority projects during the semester. Medium/Low go on a backlog for summer break.

### 4. **Leverage Downtime**

**Commuting (1 hr/day):**
- Listen to tech podcasts (Syntax.fm, The Changelog)
- Watch YouTube tutorials (2x speed)
- Read docs on my phone

**Waiting (between classes):**
- Review Anki flashcards (for interviews)
- Skim Hacker News / dev.to
- Outline blog posts in Notes app

**Before bed (30 min):**
- Read technical books (currently: *Designing Data-Intensive Applications*)

### 5. **Learning from Mentors**

I reached out to 3 senior developers on LinkedIn. Two responded. We chat monthly.

**What I ask:**
- Code reviews for my projects
- Career advice (internships, resume)
- Which technologies to focus on

**How I found them:**
- LinkedIn (search "Software Engineer" + my city)
- GitHub (contributors to projects I use)
- Meetups and hackathons

**Tip:** Don't just ask for help. Offer value:
> "I noticed you're working on [project]. I'm learning [tech]. Happy to help with documentation or testing."

### 6. **Ship Small, Iterate**

I used to start projects and never finish them. Now I follow the **80/20 rule**:

**First version:**
- Core feature only
- Basic UI (Tailwind + shadcn/ui)
- Deploy to Vercel/Render
- Ship in 1–2 weeks

**Then iterate:**
- Add features based on feedback
- Improve UI/UX
- Optimize performance

**Examples:**
- TicketPilot v1: Just a chatbot (no search, no embeddings)
- ScrubPy v1: One function (\`clean()\`), no config options

Shipping fast keeps me motivated. Perfection is the enemy of progress.

### 7. **Avoid Burnout**

**Warning signs I watch for:**
- Staring at code without making progress
- Dreading opening my laptop
- Ignoring friends/hobbies

**When I hit burnout:**
- Take 2–3 days completely off
- Go for walks, read fiction, hang out with non-tech friends
- Come back with a smaller goal ("just fix this bug")

**What works for me:**
- Exercise 3x/week (gym or running)
- One "no laptop" day per week
- Sleep 7–8 hours (non-negotiable)

### 8. **Document Everything**

I keep a **project journal** in Notion:

**Weekly log:**
- What I built
- What I learned
- Blockers I hit
- Next week's goals

**Benefits:**
- Track progress (motivating)
- Debugging (when did X break?)
- Interview prep (stories for behavioral questions)

**Example entry:**
> **Nov 3, 2024:** Integrated FAISS into TicketPilot. Learned about vector embeddings and similarity search. Got stuck on indexing large datasets — fixed by switching to \`IndexIVFFlat\`. Next: tune search parameters for better relevance.

### 9. **Use AI Wisely**

GitHub Copilot and ChatGPT are game-changers, but:

**✅ Use for:**
- Boilerplate code
- Debugging syntax errors
- Explaining complex concepts

**❌ Don't use for:**
- Learning fundamentals (write DSA from scratch)
- Exams (obviously)
- Understanding *why* code works

### 10. **Measure Progress, Not Perfection**

Instead of "I want to be a senior engineer," I track:

**Measurable goals:**
- Ship 1 project per quarter
- Write 1 blog post per month
- Contribute to 2 open-source repos this year

**Wins this year:**
- ✅ Published ScrubPy to PyPI (1.2K downloads)
- ✅ Deployed 3 full-stack apps
- ✅ Landed a summer internship

## What I'm Still Learning

**Struggles:**
- Saying "no" to interesting side projects
- Avoiding scope creep (features keep expanding)
- Balancing social life (working on this)

**Next experiments:**
- Pomodoro technique (25 min focus, 5 min break)
- Pair programming with classmates
- Blogging publicly (forces me to clarify my thinking)

## My Advice to Other Students

### 1. **Start Small**
Don't build the next Instagram. Build:
- A todo app (seriously)
- A personal site
- A CLI tool you'd use

### 2. **Pick One Stack**
Don't learn React, Vue, Angular, and Svelte simultaneously. Master one first.

My stack:
- **Frontend:** React + TypeScript
- **Backend:** FastAPI + PostgreSQL
- **Deployment:** Docker + Render/Vercel

### 3. **Build in Public**
Tweet your progress. Write blog posts. Share on LinkedIn.

**Benefits:**
- Accountability
- Feedback from others
- Portfolio for recruiters

### 4. **Join Communities**
- Discord servers (The Primeagen, Theo, etc.)
- Reddit (r/webdev, r/cscareerquestions)
- Local meetups

### 5. **Internships > Grades**
A 3.5 GPA + 2 internships > 4.0 GPA + 0 experience.

Grades matter for first internship, but after that, projects and experience win.

## Resources I Use

**Productivity:**
- Notion (project tracking)
- Google Calendar (time blocking)
- Pomodoro timers (Forest app)

**Learning:**
- freeCodeCamp (project-based)
- Frontend Masters (deep dives)
- YouTube (Fireship, Web Dev Simplified)

**Community:**
- Dev.to (reading/writing)
- Hacker News (industry news)
- Twitter (tech threads)

## Final Thoughts

You don't need to sacrifice your grades or your projects. With intentional time management and smart prioritization, you can do both.

**Key takeaways:**
1. Align projects with coursework
2. Ship small, iterate fast
3. Prioritize ruthlessly
4. Avoid burnout
5. Measure progress

You've got this! 🚀

---

*What works for you? I'd love to hear your strategies. Hit me up on [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/dhanush-ranga-gopisetty-0a910b123/)!*`,
    coverImage: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434030216411-0b793f4b4173?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop",
    category: { id: "3", name: "Career", slug: "career" },
    tags: ["student-life", "projects", "productivity", "learning"],
    featured: false,
